<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Archbishopric of Bremen</h1><p class="st-meta">1180 CE – 1648 CE (approx.) · capital Bremervörde</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Archbishopric of the North Sea coast, heir to Ansgar's mission to Scandinavia, which ruled the land between the Weser and Elbe until Sweden took it in 1648.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The archdiocese of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Hamburg</a> was founded in 831 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ansgar">Ansgar</a>, apostle of the North, and joined to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Bremen</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> in 845. Under Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adalbert-of-hamburg">Adalbert</a> in the 11th century it claimed authority over all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> fell in 1180 the archbishops built a secular principality between the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The archbishops governed marshland and moor from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stade">Stade</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremervorde">Bremervörde</a>, while the wealthy city of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Bremen</a> slipped from their control. Free peasant communities in the marshes resisted lordship. The estates, including the cathedral chapter, nobles and towns, shared power. In the 16th century the land became Lutheran, and administrators came from princely houses such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-holstein-gottorp">Holstein-Gottorp</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 secularised it as the Duchy of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Bremen</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, which ruled it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verden">Verden</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> took it in 1712 and sold it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-hanover">Electorate of Hanover</a> in 1715. The land is now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-archbishopric-of-bremen.jpg" alt="Prince-Archbishopric of Bremen" width="320" height="272"><figcaption>Image: Frederik de Wit · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bremen-Verden1655.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1180 CE – 1648 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bremervörde</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Adalbert of Bremen, Christopher of Brunswick-Lüneburg, John Frederick of Holstein-Gottorp</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, then Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Archbishopric of Hamburg-Bremen, Erzstift Bremen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1414&amp;focus=prince-archbishopric-of-bremen" aria-label="Prince-Archbishopric of Bremen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4883 339 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Archbishopric of Bremen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5203 595L5229 694M5203 595L5211 592"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5211 592h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5203 595h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5203 595h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bremervörde. <a href="/atlas?year=1414&amp;focus=prince-archbishopric-of-bremen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
